Interaction and Control of an “AVF Terminal” from an Android App

Executive summary

The term “AVF terminal” is materially ambiguous, and the integration strategy changes completely depending on what “AVF” means. The strongest official match I found is Android Virtualization Framework (AVF): Android now ships a Linux Terminal experience backed by an AVF virtual machine. In that architecture, the Android Terminal app launches and manages a VM, discovers the guest’s ttyd terminal service, and loads the service in a WebView; current AOSP code discovers a service named ttyd of type _http._tcp and connects to its advertised IP address and port. AVF itself exposes VM-management APIs and a host↔guest communication path using Binder over vsock. citeturn228017search0turn228017search6turn228017search9turn614097search1

This interpretation is substantially more plausible than Apple AVFoundation. Apple’s AVFoundation is an audiovisual-media framework for Apple platforms, not an Android terminal-control or payment-terminal protocol. citeturn752695search0turn752695search1 I did not find authoritative primary documentation for a payment-terminal product family literally branded “AVF,” so a payment-device interpretation should not be locked in until the actual manufacturer and model are known.

If the target really is the Android AVF Linux Terminal, a normal third-party Android application cannot simply instantiate or control protected AVF VMs in the same way the system Terminal app does. AOSP states that only apps signed with the platform key can obtain permission to create, own, or interact with protected VMs, and the Terminal app itself requests privileged permissions including MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE and USE_CUSTOM_VIRTUAL_MACHINE. citeturn614097search0turn614097search2 Consequently, the robust architecture is either: a platform/OEM-signed application using the AVF Java APIs directly; or a privileged broker/service exposing a narrow IPC interface to an ordinary application. Trying to automate the stock Terminal application’s WebView or depend on undocumented ttyd internals is much less stable.

If “AVF terminal” instead means a payment terminal, the integration should generally use the vendor’s SDK or Terminal API rather than sending raw EMV commands. Current mainstream models illustrate the pattern: Stripe Terminal supports Android SDK integration with Bluetooth, USB on Android for supported mobile readers, and internet-connected readers; Adyen supports local HTTPS Terminal API calls, cloud communication via terminal-established WebSockets, and Android apps running directly on certain terminals; Square exposes Android APIs for Bluetooth, USB, audio and embedded/Tap-to-Pay readers. citeturn207997search0turn825946search0turn825946search17turn825946search6

The engineering recommendation is therefore to introduce a terminal-adapter abstraction above all physical transports. Keep transaction state, retries, idempotency, security policy and telemetry independent of USB/Bluetooth/TCP, and implement either an AvfVmAdapter or a vendor-specific payment adapter beneath it. That prevents an early misunderstanding of “AVF” from contaminating the rest of the application architecture.

Meaning of AVF and likely terminal types

Android Virtualization Framework Terminal

Android’s AVF provides isolated VM execution using components including pKVM, crosvm, VirtualizationService and Microdroid/custom guests. AVF is supported only on ARM64 devices, and its Java APIs are present only on devices supporting the framework. citeturn228017search0

Google’s Linux Terminal implementation uses AVF to create a Debian-based VM. The official architecture describes the Android Terminal application, the AVF VM, a guest agent and ttyd; the Terminal application connects a WebView to a web service inside the VM. citeturn785603search0turn228017search1 Current AOSP code goes further: it searches with Android NsdManager for an _http._tcp service named ttyd, extracts its IP address and port, and loads that URL into the terminal view. citeturn228017search6turn614097search8

Earlier source revisions connected to port 7681 after obtaining the VM IP address, and the current Terminal application still contains explicit terminal service handling around ttyd. citeturn228017search2turn228017search4 These implementation details are useful for understanding the architecture, but they should not be treated as a stable public protocol contract unless Google documents them as such.

The lower-level, supported AVF communication model is significantly cleaner: a host application creates or obtains a VirtualMachine, starts it, waits for the payload-ready callback and connects to a service in the guest with connectToVsockServer(), typically exposing a custom AIDL service over the returned Binder channel. citeturn228017search9 VirtualizationService documents vsock as the primary VM communication mechanism and notes that the VM context identifier can be recycled after termination, so code should not persist CIDs as durable endpoint identities. citeturn614097search1

Apple AVFoundation

Apple AVFoundation covers audiovisual assets, media capture, playback, audio, cameras and media processing on Apple operating systems. It is not an Android interface and is not an external terminal-control API. citeturn752695search0turn752695search1 If someone internally abbreviates an audiovisual appliance or system as “AVF,” that would be a project-specific acronym and requires manufacturer documentation before protocol assumptions can be made.

Payment-terminal interpretation

No primary vendor documentation I found establishes a payment-terminal brand or model family simply called “AVF.” For comparison, mainstream vendors explicitly name their integration surfaces and hardware. Stripe lists readers such as WisePOS E, Stripe Reader S700/S710/M2 and WisePad 3 depending on region, and exposes the Stripe Terminal SDK. citeturn207997search1 Adyen exposes its Terminal API and supports local or cloud communications. citeturn825946search7turn825946search11 Square’s Mobile Payments SDK exposes reader information including model, serial number, firmware, supported entry methods and connection type. citeturn825946search6 Handpoint supports Android integrations for devices including HiLite, multiple PAX Android terminals and Telpo TPS900. citeturn785603search10turn785603search11

Accordingly, for an unknown “AVF payment terminal,” the first integration milestone should be device identification, not protocol implementation: capture manufacturer, exact model, USB VID/PID if applicable, Bluetooth advertised name/service UUIDs, firmware version, network discovery behavior and any SDK package installed on the terminal.

