The architectural distinction is important: transport and command protocol are separate layers. USB Host gives Android endpoint access, Bluetooth RFCOMM gives a byte stream, BLE gives GATT characteristics, and TCP gives sockets; none of those defines the terminal’s business commands. For an unknown terminal you still need the manufacturer’s framing, command identifiers, checksums, timeouts, state machine and security requirements.

For AVF specifically, the command layer should preferably be your own AIDL/RPC service in the guest rather than terminal keystrokes. AOSP’s supported AVF application example creates a VM, waits for onPayloadReady, connects through vsock and invokes an AIDL method. citeturn228017search9 That gives typed requests, structured errors and explicit authorization instead of scraping a shell.

A minimal Java-style AVF host flow is structurally:

VirtualMachineConfig config =  
    new VirtualMachineConfig.Builder(context, "assets/vm_config.json").build();  
  
VirtualMachine vm =  
    VirtualMachineManager.getInstance(context)  
        .getOrCreate("terminal_vm", config);  
  
vm.setCallback(executor, new VirtualMachineCallback() {  
    @Override  
    public void onPayloadReady(VirtualMachine vm) {  
        try {  
            IBinder binder = vm.connectToVsockServer(PORT).get();  
            ITerminalControl svc = ITerminalControl.Stub.asInterface(binder);  
  
            TerminalResult result =  
                svc.execute(new TerminalCommand("status"));  
        } catch (Exception e) {  
            // map to domain error / trigger recovery  
        }  
    }  
});  
  
vm.run();

This pattern follows the official AVF host↔guest API shape; it is not, however, generally available to an ordinary Play-distributed third-party application because of AVF’s privilege model. citeturn228017search9turn614097search2

A stock-Terminal discovery prototype could mirror the current AOSP mechanism:

val nsd = getSystemService(NsdManager::class.java)  
  
val info = NsdServiceInfo().apply {  
    serviceType = "_http._tcp"  
    serviceName = "ttyd"  
}  
  
nsd.registerServiceInfoCallback(  
    info,  
    executor,  
    object : NsdManager.ServiceInfoCallback {  
        override fun onServiceUpdated(service: NsdServiceInfo) {  
            val host = service.hostAddresses.firstOrNull() ?: return  
            val endpoint = "http://${host.hostAddress}:${service.port}"  
            // Prefer using this only for diagnostics/UI integration,  
            // not as a durable command API.  
        }  
  
        override fun onServiceLost() {}  
        override fun onServiceInfoCallbackRegistrationFailed(errorCode: Int) {}  
        override fun onServiceInfoCallbackUnregistered() {}  
    }  
)

That shape reflects current Terminal application source using NsdManager and the ttyd service. citeturn228017search6 On modern Android, mDNS/local-network permissions need careful version handling; Android 17/API 37 adds ACCESS_LOCAL_NETWORK restrictions and allows user-approved per-service access through newer NsdManager discovery flows. citeturn614097search7

For a generic Bluetooth Classic terminal, Android’s supported stream transport is RFCOMM through BluetoothSocket; an unpaired device can trigger the system pairing dialog automatically during connection. citeturn808427search13turn808427search26 A transport adapter can therefore be as simple as:

class RfcommTransport(  
    private val device: BluetoothDevice,  
    private val serviceUuid: UUID  
) {  
    private var socket: BluetoothSocket? = null  
  
    fun connect() {  
        val s = device.createRfcommSocketToServiceRecord(serviceUuid)  
        s.connect()  
        socket = s  
    }  
  
    fun send(frame: ByteArray): ByteArray {  
        val s = requireNotNull(socket)  
        s.outputStream.write(frame)  
        s.outputStream.flush()  
  
        val buffer = ByteArray(4096)  
        val n = s.inputStream.read(buffer)  
        return buffer.copyOf(n)  
    }  
  
    fun close() {  
        socket?.close()  
        socket = null  
    }  
}

The actual frame format must come from the terminal manufacturer. Android only supplies the RFCOMM pipe. citeturn808427search13turn808427search31

For USB serial devices, Android USB Host supplies raw device/interface/endpoint access; a widely used open-source Java library, usb-serial-for-android, adds CDC/ACM and common converter drivers such as FTDI, PL2303, CP210x and CH34x without root. citeturn785603search5turn808427search0 It should be treated as a transport library rather than a payment or AVF SDK.

Android permissions, pairing and connection lifecycle

For AVF, the stock Terminal manifest requests MANAGE_VIRTUAL_MACHINE, USE_CUSTOM_VIRTUAL_MACHINE, INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, foreground-service permissions, notification permission and other capabilities. It also declares android.software.virtualization_framework as a required feature. citeturn614097search0 The key limitation is not ordinary runtime permission handling but privileged authorization: AOSP says platform-key-signed apps are the ones that can request permission to create, own or interact with pVMs. citeturn614097search2

For USB Host, declare the USB host feature and either listen for USB_DEVICE_ATTACHED with a VID/PID filter or enumerate UsbManager.deviceList. Android requires user permission before opening a USB device unless permission was granted through handling the attachment intent. citeturn785603search3turn785603search6 A robust workflow is attach → identify VID/PID/interfaces → request permission → openDevice() → claim interface → start I/O → handle detach → close all endpoints and reset connection state.

For Bluetooth on Android 12/API 31 and later, scanning and connecting use the nearby-device permission model, notably BLUETOOTH_SCAN and BLUETOOTH_CONNECT; Android 11 and below used legacy Bluetooth permissions and could require fine location for scanning. citeturn785603search4turn808427search15 Bluetooth Classic terminals generally follow scan → select → system pair/bond → RFCOMM connect → protocol handshake. BLE terminals use connectGatt() and a GATT state machine rather than a byte-stream socket. citeturn808427search22turn808427search26

For NFC, Android exposes NfcAdapter, reader mode and host card emulation. Reader mode can be enabled for a foreground activity through enableReaderMode(). citeturn808427search37turn808427search38 Payment applications should not assume this gives them access to an EMV payment kernel: in certified payment solutions, card data acquisition and kernel processing are typically owned by the vendor’s certified SDK or payment application rather than by arbitrary app NFC code. EMVCo separates Level 1 interface/protocol testing from Level 2 kernel/software testing. citeturn602845search5turn602845search9

For TCP/IP, the application typically needs INTERNET; local terminal discovery may additionally involve NsdManager, Wi-Fi APIs or Android’s local-network permission model depending on target API level. Starting with API 37, Android restricts local-network access and provides explicit user-approved discovery paths for services discovered using NsdManager. citeturn614097search7

For Android Enterprise deployments, a dedicated terminal/controller device can be fully managed, locked into an allowlisted set of apps and remotely configured. Android Enterprise supports dedicated-device/kiosk deployment and managed configurations, which are useful for provisioning terminal IDs, environment endpoints, retry policies and feature flags without embedding them in the APK. citeturn597525search0turn597525search1turn597525search6

A connection state machine should be explicit:

stateDiagram-v2  
    [*] --> Idle  
    Idle --> Discovering: connect()  
    Discovering --> PermissionRequired: permission missing  
    PermissionRequired --> Discovering: granted  
    PermissionRequired --> Failed: denied  
  
    Discovering --> Pairing: Bluetooth unbonded  
    Discovering --> Connecting: endpoint found  
    Pairing --> Connecting: bonded  
    Pairing --> Failed: pairing timeout/failure  
  
    Connecting --> Authenticating: transport open  
    Authenticating --> Ready: protocol/session accepted  
    Authenticating --> Backoff: handshake failed  
  
    Ready --> Busy: command/transaction  
    Busy --> Ready: completed  
    Busy --> Recovering: timeout/link loss  
    Ready --> Recovering: disconnect  
  
    Recovering --> Backoff  
    Backoff --> Discovering: retry budget remains  
    Backoff --> Failed: retry budget exhausted  
  
    Ready --> Idle: explicit disconnect  
    Failed --> Idle: reset

This model maps cleanly both to AVF guest-service connections and external terminals; only the discovery, authentication and transport-specific stages change.

Security, payment certification and platform controls

If the target is Android AVF, its most important security property is isolation. AVF is designed to offer stronger isolation than the normal Android application sandbox, and protected VMs are intended to preserve guest confidentiality even if the host Android environment is compromised. citeturn228017search0 Host↔guest access should therefore be deliberately narrow: expose only the operations needed by the application, avoid a generic root shell API, authenticate every high-value RPC and define explicit message schemas.

The Android host itself is already constrained by the application sandbox and SELinux mandatory access control. Android uses SELinux to enforce access control even against processes with root capabilities, and third-party applications execute within separate app security domains. citeturn768854search1turn768854search12 A normal application should not attempt to weaken SELinux policy to gain hardware or VM access; if OEM-level integration requires a vendor daemon, define a minimal SELinux domain and narrowly scoped device-node/Binder permissions.

For secrets, Android Keystore can keep key material non-exportable, with hardware-backed TEE or StrongBox protection where available. StrongBox offers stronger isolation for supported devices. citeturn768854search0turn768854search24 Use it for terminal-client credentials, API client certificates, device identity keys and local data-encryption keys rather than storing private keys in resources or shared preferences.

FIPS is conditional rather than universally required. FIPS 140-3 applies when the deployment contract, regulator or government security profile requires validated cryptography. NIST’s CMVP validates specific cryptographic modules and their documented operating environments; using “AES” or the Android Keystore does not automatically make an application FIPS validated. citeturn768854search14turn768854search18 If FIPS is required, select a device/OS/module combination covered by a current CMVP certificate and ensure all relevant cryptographic services remain inside the validated boundary.

If the terminal is a payment terminal, the compliance model is more demanding. PCI PTS covers point-of-interaction devices, while PCI DSS governs protection of payment account data in the merchant environment; use of a PTS-approved device alone does not automatically remove the integrating system from PCI DSS scope. citeturn785603search13turn785603search15 PCI SSC explicitly notes that SRED-capable PTS devices and validated P2PE solutions can help reduce scope, but an assessor should not assume a terminal is encrypting cardholder data merely because it is PTS approved. citeturn785603search15

For payment acceptance directly on commercial Android hardware, PCI MPoC is the strategic standard. MPoC covers mobile payment acceptance on commercial off-the-shelf devices and combines use cases involving contactless cardholder data and PIN entry. citeturn597525search2 PCI announced a sunset period for the older SPoC and CPoC programs from May 1 through October 31, 2026; existing listings remain valid through their listing lifecycle, but new submissions cease after the sunset period. citeturn597525search31

EMV certification is similarly layered. EMV Level 1 evaluates the physical/electrical/contactless communications interface; Level 2 evaluates the payment application/kernel behavior; Level 3 validates the integrated acceptance-device-to-host/acquirer transaction flow under payment-system test plans. citeturn602845search5turn602845search10 An Android application using an already certified vendor SDK normally relies on the vendor/acquirer’s approved kernel rather than implementing raw EMV APDUs itself.

The payment-security boundary should therefore look like:

