vsock/AIDL  vendor SDK  USB/BT/TCP

TransactionCoordinator should own business idempotency, user-visible progress and recovery. The adapter should own protocol framing, transport-specific timeouts and mapping of vendor/VM events into a normalized domain model. This separation is especially valuable here because “AVF” has not yet been resolved to one physical product.

For an AVF implementation, the preferred control path is:

sequenceDiagram  
    participant App as Android App  
    participant Broker as Privileged AVF Broker  
    participant AVF as VirtualMachine / AVF  
    participant Guest as Guest Control Service  
  
    App->>Broker: execute(command, requestId)  
    Broker->>AVF: getOrCreate(vm)  
    Broker->>AVF: run()  
    AVF-->>Broker: onPayloadReady()  
    Broker->>Guest: connectToVsockServer(port)  
    Guest-->>Broker: Binder/AIDL interface  
    Broker->>Guest: execute(command, requestId)  
    Guest-->>Broker: structured result  
    Broker-->>App: sanitized result

This is preferable to typing shell commands into ttyd because the AVF Java API already provides a supported VM-to-service communication mechanism through vsock and Binder. citeturn228017search9turn614097search1 The stock ttyd/WebView path is appropriate for human terminal interaction, but it is an implementation surface rather than a durable automation contract. That is an engineering inference from the current AOSP architecture and source. citeturn228017search6turn228017search7

For payment transactions:

sequenceDiagram  
    participant UI as Android POS  
    participant TC as Transaction Coordinator  
    participant SDK as Vendor Terminal Adapter  
    participant T as Payment Terminal  
    participant PSP as PSP / Acquirer  
  
    UI->>TC: pay(amount, orderId)  
    TC->>TC: create idempotency key  
    TC->>SDK: ensureConnected()  
    SDK->>T: initiate payment  
    T-->>SDK: present/tap/insert card requested  
    SDK-->>TC: AwaitingCard  
    T->>PSP: authorize transaction  
    PSP-->>T: approved / declined  
    T-->>SDK: final terminal result  
    SDK-->>TC: normalized result + PSP reference  
    TC->>TC: persist final state atomically  
    TC-->>UI: Approved / Declined / Unknown

The most dangerous error state is unknown outcome, not an ordinary failure. If a socket disappears after the terminal submitted a transaction but before the Android app received its response, blindly repeating the payment can double-charge the customer. Recovery must query transaction status using the same merchant reference/idempotency identifier before retrying a financial operation.

For reconnection, use bounded exponential backoff with jitter for transport failures, but do not apply generic automatic retry to irreversible commands. A practical policy is immediate retries only for discovery/connection establishment; status-query retry for ambiguous transactions; and explicit operator intervention after a bounded number of failures. Square’s SDK, for example, exposes reader state and a dedicated retryConnection() API rather than requiring applications to reconstruct the Bluetooth protocol themselves. citeturn825946search6turn825946search9

For AVF VM failure, monitor the VM callback lifecycle and assume a VM can terminate when its controlling references disappear; VirtualizationService documents that losing all references to the VM handle can cause the VM to be shut down. citeturn614097search1 Persist application-level task state outside the VM if commands must survive host process death.

Performance-wise, local Binder/vsock or on-device app-to-app communication minimizes network round trips; network payment integrations add LAN or WAN latency and availability dependencies. Adyen explicitly distinguishes local communications, where the POS sends requests directly to the terminal on the LAN, from cloud communications, where the POS interacts through Adyen’s cloud. citeturn825946search0turn825946search7 The most important latency metric is therefore not raw transport RTT but end-to-end transaction percentile latency, split into connection setup, terminal interaction, card processing, authorization and final-result propagation.

For BLE, minimize unnecessary reconnects and GATT discovery; for Bluetooth Classic, keep a connection open during a transaction; for USB, avoid opening and closing the interface per command; and for TCP/TLS, reuse connections when supported. Those are engineering recommendations rather than vendor-specific protocol requirements.

Testing strategy, implementation roadmap and compliance checklist

Testing should be layered. At the lowest level, validate frame encoding/decoding with deterministic unit tests and golden vectors. Add transport tests for USB attach/detach, permission denial, Bluetooth disabled, radio loss, pairing cancellation, network change and terminal reboot. Add transaction-state tests for timeout before submission, timeout after submission, duplicate responses, delayed responses and application process death. For AVF, exercise VM boot failure, payload-not-ready, guest-service disappearance, CID changes and broker death. AOSP’s vm debugging tooling can start, inspect and terminate VMs, while debuggable AVF guests expose richer logs and ADB access; production VMs should use non-debuggable mode. citeturn614097search1

For Android Enterprise deployments, test under the actual EMM/device-owner policy because work profiles and fully managed devices can alter intent routing, application availability and device behavior. Android explicitly recommends end-to-end testing with the target EMM for dedicated-device solutions. citeturn597525search6 For payment vendors, use their supported simulator/sandbox when available; Square, for example, provides a Mock Reader UI because physical readers cannot be exercised inside the Square Sandbox. citeturn825946search6

A sensible implementation roadmap is four milestones. Discovery and proof of identity should establish what AVF means, identify exact hardware/software version, determine whether the application can obtain privileged AVF access or must use a vendor SDK, and capture all available protocol documentation. Transport proof of concept should establish one reliable end-to-end connection and one harmless command such as status/version. Production state machine should add idempotency, structured errors, reconnect/recovery, telemetry, secure credential storage, lifecycle handling and automated fault tests. Certification and deployment should complete vendor/acquirer certification, PCI/EMV obligations where relevant, Android Enterprise provisioning, signed production releases, observability dashboards and rollout controls.

The resulting go-live checklist should cover the following:

- Identity and interface: exact vendor/model/firmware recorded; supported SDK/API version pinned; command protocol and framing obtained from an authoritative source; no undocumented shell/UI automation in the critical path.
- Android permissions: USB permission and detach handling tested where relevant; BLUETOOTH_SCAN/BLUETOOTH_CONNECT handled on modern Android; legacy permission behavior tested on supported older versions; local-network/API-37 behavior accounted for; NFC capability explicitly detected before use. citeturn785603search3turn785603search4turn614097search7turn808427search37
- AVF security: direct VM management used only by an appropriately privileged/platform-signed component; guest service uses a narrow AIDL/RPC contract; production VM is non-debuggable; no reliance on persistent VM CIDs. citeturn614097search2turn614097search1
- Secrets: private keys and credentials stored through Android Keystore; StrongBox requested where required and supported; secrets never logged; certificate rotation and expiry monitoring defined. citeturn768854search0turn768854search24
- Network security: TLS/server identity validation enabled for network terminals; local terminal certificates validated where vendor architecture requires it; no permissive trust manager; sensitive commands authenticated. Adyen’s local integration, for example, requires terminal-certificate validation and encrypted communication using the configured shared key. citeturn825946search23
- Payment scope: PAN, track data and PIN never exposed to the Android business application unless the certified architecture explicitly requires it; PTS/P2PE/PCI DSS scope confirmed with the acquirer/QSA; MPoC applicability checked for COTS/Tap-to-Pay deployments. citeturn785603search15turn597525search2
- EMV: approved L1/L2 components used where card acceptance is involved; L3/acquirer certification completed for the intended processor, region, payment methods and terminal configuration. citeturn602845search5turn602845search10
- FIPS: treated as a contractual/regulatory requirement, not assumed; if required, the exact cryptographic module and operating environment are verified against the NIST CMVP listing. citeturn768854search18
- Android hardening: SELinux remains enforcing; devices are not rooted; terminal/POS software is deployed through controlled signing and management; Android Enterprise kiosk/dedicated-device controls are used where operationally appropriate. citeturn768854search1turn597525search6
- Reliability: every command has timeout classification; reconnect uses bounded backoff and jitter; transaction IDs are durable; an “unknown outcome” triggers status reconciliation rather than an automatic duplicate payment.
- Observability: logs contain terminal model, firmware, connection state, request ID, latency buckets and normalized error code, but no cardholder or authentication secrets.
- Testing: permission denial, device detach, radio loss, terminal reboot, app process death, network partition, duplicated callback, delayed callback and ambiguous transaction completion all have automated or scripted test cases.
- Deployment: app signing keys protected; rollback path tested; firmware/SDK compatibility matrix maintained; production rollout staged by site or device cohort; vendor SDK end-of-support dates monitored.

Primary documentation and decision guidance

For an Android AVF Terminal implementation, the authoritative starting points are Google’s AVF overview, the official “Write an AVF app” guide, VirtualizationService documentation, AVF security documentation and current AOSP Terminal application source. Together they document VM creation, vsock/Binder communication, privilege requirements and the Terminal app’s ttyd/NSD architecture. citeturn228017search0turn228017search9turn614097search1turn614097search2turn228017search6

For generic Android connectivity, the primary references are Android’s USB Host documentation, Bluetooth permissions and RFCOMM/GATT APIs, NFC APIs, NsdManager, Android Keystore and Android Enterprise documentation. citeturn785603search3turn785603search4turn808427search13turn808427search22turn808427search37turn614097search7turn768854search0turn597525search0

For a payment-terminal interpretation, the integration should begin from the vendor’s certified surface rather than from raw USB/Bluetooth. Stripe Terminal exposes SDK-driven reader control and pre-certified readers; Adyen exposes its Terminal API with local and cloud architectures; Square’s Mobile Payments SDK manages pairing, reader state and payment operations; Handpoint exposes dedicated Android SDK paths for supported PAX/Telpo/HiLite hardware. citeturn207997search0turn825946search7turn825946search6turn785603search10

The key architectural conclusion is therefore conditional but clear: if AVF means Android Virtualization Framework, use privileged AVF APIs and a purpose-built Binder/vsock service; if AVF means a payment terminal, identify the exact vendor/model and use its supported SDK or Terminal API; if it is merely a generic serial/Bluetooth appliance, isolate the transport behind a terminal adapter and obtain the manufacturer’s command specification before writing control logic. This approach preserves the Android application architecture while the acronym ambiguity is resolved and minimizes both security and certification risk.